# {{ .Project }}

ES-tier project generated with aspgen's --context/--arch engine. Aggregates
are event-sourced: state is derived entirely by replaying domain events from
an append-only event store, and queries read from a synchronously-maintained
projection (read model) instead of the aggregate's own table.

Layers: DomainModel, Application, Infrastructure, Persistence, Resources, WebApi.

## Build

From the generated project root:

```powershell
dotnet restore .\{{ .Project }}.sln
dotnet build .\{{ .Project }}.sln
```

## Run

```powershell
dotnet run --project src\WebApi
```

## CI / production build

`.\scripts\ci.ps1` runs restore, build, and test in one step, and can publish
the WebApi host with `-Publish` (skip tests with `-SkipTests`, override
configuration with `-Configuration Debug`):

```powershell
.\scripts\ci.ps1 -Publish
```

Add aggregates to the initial bounded context, or add another context:

```powershell
aspgen add aggregate Product name:string price:decimal active:bool --context {{ .Namespace }} --project .
aspgen add context Billing --arch es --project .
```

Each aggregate gets: a `{Aggregate}CreatedEvent`/`UpdatedEvent`/`DeletedEvent`
trio, an event-sourced aggregate class (`{Aggregate}.LoadFromHistory`,
`Create`/`Update`/`Delete` raising events), a `{Aggregate}EventStoreRepository`
(load by replay, save by appending + projecting), vertical-slice
Command/Query/Handler records, a `{Aggregate}ReadModel` projection table, and
Minimal API endpoints mounted at `/api/{context}/{aggregate}` in
`src/WebApi/Program.cs`.

Event versioning/schema evolution and snapshotting are out of scope for the
generated scaffold; add them by hand as the event store grows.
